/**
 * The unordered counterparts of the built-in `foreach` and `reduce`: the scheduler may run the
 * calls of the function in any order and at the same time.
 */

// The declarations of the operators of the C++ module `modules/para` (design principles 6.4;
// handover H19, step 10). This file is the one source of their signatures; the C++ side keeps the
// kernels, the executor, the operator traits and the resources. `rendezvous` names
// `para:rendezvous` by convention, so no `#op` tag is needed; the two unordered operators are
// built-in element calls (`:mark/...`), so each carries the exact URI of its operator.
//
// `unordered_foreach` and `unordered_reduce` take the element type of their array argument as the
// type of the function they call, and `unordered_foreach` does not constrain what that function
// returns: `(x: int) => x` is a valid body for it, as `test/cases/para/core/map_chunks.cml`
// shows. The callback's result is therefore a type variable too (the head parameter `R`), not
// `void`: the declaration reproduces the old C++ resolver's accept set (handover H194,
// corrected by H202).

module para

/// Blocks until `parties` calls are waiting at the one meeting point of the process, or until
/// timeout_ms milliseconds have passed; returns how many calls met (after a timeout, how many
/// were waiting). A tool for showing that calls really run at the same time.
/// @effect waits for other calls
/// @fails fault `RuntimeError` when parties is below 1 or timeout_ms is negative
/// @example rendezvous(2, 1000)    // 2 when another call arrived in time
export func rendezvous(parties: int, timeout_ms: int): int

/// Calls f for every element; the calls have no order among them.
/// @effect what f does
/// @example [1, 2]->unordered_foreach<(x: int) => println(x)>
#op<':mark/unordered_foreach_arr'>
with <f: (item: T) => R>
export func unordered_foreach::<T, R>(xs: T[]): void

/// Combines the elements with f starting from initial; the order in which elements are
/// combined is not fixed, so f should be associative and commutative.
/// @effect what f does
/// @example [1, 2, 3]->unordered_reduce<(acc: int, x: int): int => acc + x, 0>
#op<':mark/unordered_reduce_arr'>
with <f: (acc: T, item: T) => T, initial: T>
export func unordered_reduce::<T>(xs: T[]): T
